From 4a1d7e9c2b8f5036e17c4d92ab53f60c8d1e2743 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: wire the CPU clusters to the CPU-PLL
 controller
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add the CPU-PLL clock controller node (CPU_PLL_CFG @ 0x08870000) and give
every cpu@N a `clocks` property. This is the DT half; the driver is
patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu`. It is one of the items patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology` deferred, in its own words:
"cpupll_ccu node (CPU PLL block @ 0x08870000, separate from main CCU) ...
Belongs with the cpufreq patch (foundation range); cpu@N `clocks=<>` will
need extending when that lands."

THE CLOCK CHAIN, END TO END

  DCXO 26 MHz  (RTC-CCU XO_CTRL0 @0x07090160, gate = CLK_RTC_DCXO, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`)
    -> CPU_L_PLL   @0x08871000   f = 26 * N / P / (M0*M1)
    -> EXT_DIVP    @0x0887101C bits[17:16]   /1 /2 /4
    -> mux         @0x0887101C bits[26:24]   ---> CPU0_5_CLK  -> cores 0..5
       other mux inputs: CPU_SLOW_CLK (000/001/010), PERI0PLL_DIV2 (100),
       CPU_BACK_PLL (101)

  same structure at 0x08872000 / 0x0887201C -> CPU6_7_CLK -> cores 6..7
  same structure at 0x08873000 / 0x0887301C -> DSU_CLK    -> the DSU/L3,
       plus CPU_AXI = DSU_CLK/M, CPU_GIC = DSU_CLK/M1, CPU_APB = DSU_CLK/N

The distribution is UM 5.1.4.1 "PLL Distribution":
core0..core5 <- CPU_L_PLL, core6/core7 <- CPU_B_PLL, cluster <- CPU_DSU_PLL.
The register layout is UM 5.1.5.5 "CPU_PLL_CFG Register List"
plus the per-register sections for CPU_L, CPU_B, CPU_DSU and
CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL.

WHY THE NODE POINTS AT THE MUX AND NOT AT THE PLL

The vendor DT (BSP sun60iw2p1.dtsi:56) writes
`clocks = <&cpupll_ccu CLK_PLL_CPU_L>` -- the raw PLL -- even though its own
driver also registers the mux. We point at the mux instead, deliberately.

1. The mux output is what the UM calls the CPU clock. The CPU_L_PLL_CLK_REG
   field description ends with "CPU0_5_CLK = Clock Source"
   and CPU_B_PLL_CLK_REG with "CPU6_7_CLK = Clock Source"
   (A733 user manual). The PLL is one of five sources feeding it. A DT is
   a description of hardware; the node that describes "the clock this core
   runs on" is the mux output.

2. Only the mux is documented as safe to switch under load. Both register
   descriptions state that the mux switches glitch-free and supports
   dynamic reconfiguration. The PLL carries no such statement; on the
   contrary, UM 5.1.4.2 (pre-divider frequency modulation)
   says the PLL has to be re-locked whenever a pre-divider
   changes, and step 1 of that sequence is to clear
   PLL_OUTPUT_GATE. Gating the output of a PLL that is the sole clock of a running core
   stops that core. Naming the mux is what makes the safe sequence
   expressible in the clk framework: park the mux on CPU_SLOW_CLK or
   CPU_BACK_PLL, re-lock, switch back -- exactly what
   ccu_mux_notifier_register() / ccu_mux_notifier_cb() do
   (drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu_mux.c:286-312).

3. Nothing is lost for DVFS. A mux registered with CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT
   forwards the rate request to its active parent, so clk_set_rate() on
   CLK_CPU_L still lands on CPU_L_PLL. Mainline sunxi already does exactly
   this: sun50i-a100.dtsi:26 and sun50i-h616.dtsi:28 give cpu@N
   `clocks = <&ccu CLK_CPUX>`, and CLK_CPUX is a SUNXI_CCU_MUX
   (ccu-sun50i-a100.c:267, ccu-sun50i-h616.c:251), paired with
   ccu_mux_notifier_register() on pll-cpux.

4. clk_get_rate() on the mux is the only reading that is true at all times.
   Pointed at the PLL it reports the PLL whatever the mux is doing -- and
   the mux is not always on the PLL: it has a documented parked state, and
   whether EXT_DIVP (which sits on the mux input branch, in the CLK register,
   not in the PLL) is folded into the PLL clock or modelled separately is a
   driver-internal choice that the DT should not have to depend on. A previous
   measurement showed what a wrong frequency reading looks like in practice:
   scaling_cur_freq said 2002 MHz while CPU_B_PLL held N=32 (416 MHz).

WHAT LINUX DOES WITH cpu@N's clocks[0]

Checked in the kernel source before writing. The
property is load-bearing for two subsystems:

  * drivers/base/arch_topology.c:294 -- topology_parse_cpu_capacity() calls
    of_clk_get(cpu_node, 0) and, on success, sets
    per_cpu(capacity_freq_ref) = clk_get_rate()/1000. That value is the
    multiplier applied to capacity-dmips-mhz (`arm64-dts-a733-capacity-dmips-mhz`) in
    topology_normalize_cpu_scale() (:246,:253). Note the call site: it is
    reached from get_cpu_for_node() (:485) inside parse_dt_topology(), i.e.
    from init_cpu_topology() at arch/arm64/kernel/smp.c:779 --
    smp_prepare_cpus() time, before any platform driver probes. of_clk_get()
    will therefore return -EPROBE_DEFER here, IS_ERR_OR_NULL() skips the
    assignment, and capacity_freq_ref keeps its initial 0 which
    topology_normalize_cpu_scale() reads as `?: 1`. So this patch does NOT
    change early-boot capacities; they stay at the 454/1024 ratio `arm64-dts-a733-capacity-dmips-mhz`
    established. The real update comes later, from
    init_cpu_capacity_callback() (:400) on CPUFREQ_CREATE_POLICY, which sets
    capacity_freq_ref = policy->cpuinfo.max_freq and re-normalises. That is
    the step that will turn 454/1024 into frequency-aware capacities once
    `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` land -- and it can only happen if the clock resolves.

  * drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c:96 -- cpufreq_init() does
    clk_get(cpu_dev, NULL), which is of_clk_get(np, 0) on the same property,
    and stores it as policy->clk; set_target() (:51) then drives it through
    dev_pm_opp_set_rate(). cpufreq-dt is the driver this SoC will get:
    cpufreq-dt-platdev.c registers the platform device whenever cpu0 has
    operating-points-v2 and the root compatible is not in the blocklist, and
    "allwinner,sun60i-a733" is not in that blocklist (:110-114 lists a100,
    h6, h616, h618, h700 only). So no platdev change is needed later.

  * Energy Model: cpufreq-dt sets .register_em = cpufreq_register_em_with_opp,
    so the EM is built from the OPP table. That still needs
    dynamic-power-coefficient, which `arm64-dts-a733-capacity-dmips-mhz` deliberately left out and this
    patch does not add.

VALUES AND WHERE THEY COME FROM

  reg 0x08870000 -- UM chapter 2 memory map
    (0x08870000---0x0887FFFF, "CPU_PLL_CFG"). The block is inside CPU_SYS,
    NOT part of the main CCU at 0x02002000 -- which is precisely why the
    A733 CCU driver (`clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`) cannot provide the CPU clocks.

  parent = 26 MHz DCXO -- silicon: XO_CTRL0 @0x07090160 reads 0x983FB0F7
    byte-identical on the 6 GiB board and the 8 GiB board, DCXO_ST[15:14] = 0b10 = 26 MHz.
    Spec: UM 9.1.5.4 decodes DCXO_ST 0b10 as the 26M oscillator, and
    Table 4-2 gives the CPU PLLs a 26 MHz reference (V1.00 PDF p. 410, 41).
    The CPU_BACK_PLL default note is no cross-check: V0.92 says 975 MHz
    "when DCXO is 26MHz", V1.00 says 900 MHz "when DCXO is 24MHz" (PDF
    p. 305), for the same fields (0x40104A00: N=75, OUTPUT_DIV=1). The osc24M node
    this DT still carries is correct for the main CCU and wrong for the CPU
    PLLs; that is why the CPU-PLL controller takes its parent from
    CLK_RTC_DCXO (patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`) rather than from &osc24M.

  boot state, for the reviewer to compare against after boot (the 8 GiB board, our
    6.18.36, register readback):
      0x08871000 = 0xF8802700  N=0x27=39, P=0, M0=0 -> 26*39 = 1014 MHz
      0x0887101C = 0x03000000  mux=011 (own PLL), EXT_DIVP=00 (/1)
      0x08872000 = 0xF8802700  -> 1014 MHz  (A76 cluster, same as A55)
      0x0887201C = 0x03000000
      0x08873000 = 0xF8801E00  N=0x1E=30 -> 780 MHz  (DSU)
      0x0887301C = 0x03000105  mux=011, AXI M=2, GIC M1=2, APB N=2
                               -> AXI/GIC/APB = 390 MHz
    Both clusters sit at 1014 MHz because that is what boot0 leaves behind.

WHAT THIS PATCH MUST NOT DUPLICATE, AND DOES NOT

  `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology` cpu@N identity, reg, enable-method, cpu-map, cache node graph.
  `arm64-dts-a733-heterogeneous-pmu` the two arm-pmu nodes.
  `arm64-dts-a733-psci-idle-states` idle-states + cpu-idle-states on every cpu@N.
  `arm64-dts-a733-cache-geometry` d/i-cache-size / -line-size / -sets on every cpu@N.
  `arm64-dts-a733-capacity-dmips-mhz` capacity-dmips-mhz = 454 / 1024.
  This patch adds exactly one property per cpu@N (`clocks`) and one node.
  It does not touch capacity-dmips-mhz -- `arm64-dts-a733-capacity-dmips-mhz`'s value is a ratio and stays
  correct; what changes later is only the frequency it is multiplied by.
  It does not add operating-points-v2, cpu-supply, opp-supported-hw,
  dynamic-power-coefficient, #cooling-cells or thermal zones. Those are
  `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` and later thermal work.

DOES ANYTHING IN THE EXISTING DT ALREADY CLAIM A CPU CLOCK?

No. Checked against the tree with `clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu` to `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu` + `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-smhc-pll-peri1-clocks` to `arm64-dts-allwinner-a733-add-sd-emmc` + `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-usb2-clocks` to `usb-typec-tcpci_rt1711h-drop-revision-check` +
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-gmac0-clocks` to `net-phy-maxio-split-rj45-led-functions` + `mfd-axp20x-Constify-axp313-and-axp717-cells` to `arm64-dts-a733-enable-r-i2c1-husb311-typec` applied: no cpu@N carries a `clocks` property, and
no node in the tree carries a reg in the 0x08870000-0x0887FFFF window
(the highest soc child today is rtc_ccu at 0x07090000). The new node is
appended after it, keeping the soc block roughly address-ordered. So there
is nothing to remove and nothing to conflict with. The only new coupling is
&rtc_ccu, which `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu` already introduced and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` extends with CLK_RTC_DCXO.

THE DSU: DELIBERATELY NOT REPRESENTED HERE, AND WHY

CPU_DSU_PLL clocks the DynamIQ Shared Unit -- the L3 and the snoop filter
that all eight cores go through -- and, via the dividers in
CPU_DSU_PLL_CLK_REG, CPU_AXI, CPU_GIC and CPU_APB. Silicon has it at
780 MHz with all three dividers at /2 (390 MHz). This patch adds no node
that consumes it, for three reasons:

1. There is no consumer to write. Linux has no generic "cluster clock"
   binding a cpu@N could point at; the DSU is not a cpufreq policy. The
   vendor's own answer is a `dsufreq@0` node with
   compatible = "allwinner,dsufreq" (BSP sun60iw2p1.dtsi:487), bound there
   by the BSP's own sunxi-dsufreq driver; mainline has no such driver, so in
   this tree nothing would bind it. Adding a node that nothing binds would be
   describing our intentions, not the hardware.

2. The rail makes it a real hazard, not a free win. VDD-DSU is not its own
   supply: DCDC5 feeds VDD-CPUL
   (Cortex-A55) and the DSU as one rail. So the DSU's voltage is chosen by
   whatever the A55 cluster's OPP asks for, and cpufreq knows nothing about
   the DSU. Anything that raises the DSU frequency is only safe at the
   *lowest* voltage the A55 policy can select (800 mV on both bins we have
   measured). The absolute maximum for both rails is 1.2 V
   (DS Tab. 5-1); nothing here goes near
   it, and nothing here raises a voltage at all.

3. The DT would still not be where the decision belongs. The clock IDs
   CLK_PLL_CPU_DSU and CLK_CPU_DSU exist in the header and the controller
   exposes them; a future devfreq or interconnect consumer can reference
   them without changing this node.

   Driver-side consequence that must be handled in `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu`, flagged here
   because this DT is what makes it visible: CLK_CPU_DSU has no consumer in
   this DT, and CLK_CPU_L / CLK_CPU_B have consumers that never
   clk_prepare_enable() them (arch_topology only reads the rate; cpufreq-dt
   only sets rates). clk_disable_unused() at late_initcall would therefore
   gate them. All three muxes and all four PLLs need CLK_IS_CRITICAL.

THE 75 % QUESTION -- OPEN, AND WORTH SOMEONE'S TIME

Per UM 5.1.4.1, L3 latency depends on the DSU-to-core clock ratio, and
the UM recommends a DSU clock of at least 75 % of the fastest core's.

Today the DSU runs 780 MHz. Against the A76's top OPP of 2002 MHz that is
39 %, barely half of what the UM recommends; even against the A55's top of
1794 MHz it is 43 %. Once `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` let the cores scale, every L3 miss and
every cross-cluster snoop pays that ratio, and the two A76 cores pay it
most. 75 % of 2002 MHz would be 1502 MHz, i.e. N=58 (1508 MHz) on the
existing 26 MHz ladder.

We are NOT doing that here, and the reason is the rail in point 2 above:
DCDC5 also supplies the six A55s, so the DSU only ever sees the voltage the
A55 policy has selected, and at idle that is 800 mV. What can be said from
measurement is narrow but real: on this rail, at 800 mV, an A55 core runs
1196 MHz in both bins we have measured -- which is
suggestive but is NOT proof about the DSU, a different logic block with a
different critical path. And the UM gives no Fmax for the DSU, nor for
CPU_AXI / CPU_GIC / CPU_APB, which would go from 390 MHz to 598 MHz at
1196 MHz DSU with today's /2 dividers. The divider fields could absorb that
(AXI M supports /4, GIC M1 and APB N support /4), but
"could" is not "may".

So the honest state is: a defensible improvement almost certainly exists
between 780 MHz and 1196 MHz at unchanged voltage, and we cannot yet name
the number from the documents we have. What would close it: a documented
DSU / CPU_AXI / CPU_GIC / CPU_APB maximum frequency at 0.8 V (a datasheet
electrical table, not a vendor DT), plus a consumer that keeps the DSU
below whatever DCDC5 is currently at. Recorded here rather than left as
folklore, because the ratio is a standing performance loss and someone
should own it.

Under no circumstances is any of this to be reached by raising a rail:
VDD-CPUL/VDD-CPUB absolute maximum is 1.2 V per DS Tab. 5-1 and the highest
value in any vendor bin is 1050 mV.

DEPENDENCIES

  `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu`  rtc_ccu node + driver (already on the branch)
  `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`  CLK_RTC_DCXO = 2 in the RTC-CCU (the `clocks` parent here)
  `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu`  the CPU-PLL driver, which brings
        include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-cpu-ccu.h and the
        "allwinner,sun60i-a733-cpu-ccu" compatible

The dt-bindings header is NOT added by this patch. Repo convention is that
the header ships with the clk driver that defines the ids (`clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu` carries
sun60i-a733-ccu.h, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu` carries sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu.h), and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu` is that
driver here. If `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu` turns out not to carry it, this series fails at dtc
with "CLK_CPU_L undeclared", which is loud and cheap to fix; the alternative
-- both patches creating the same new file -- fails at apply time instead.

WHY THE MAPPING IS 0x3038 AND NOT THE VENDOR'S 0x3034

The vendor DT maps 0x3034 (sun60iw2p1.dtsi:826), which works for it because
its driver never reads the last register in the block. CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL sits
*at* offset 0x3034 (UM register list and its CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL_REG section,
bits[1:0] = 00 DCXO / 01 CLK32K / 10 CLK16M_RC), so a 0x3034-sized mapping
covers 0x0000..0x3033 and excludes exactly that register.

Our driver does read it, and must: `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu` parks the core mux on CPU_SLOW_CLK
while it reprograms a PLL, and this register decides what CPU_SLOW_CLK is.
Parking eight cores on the 32 kHz clock instead of the DCXO is not a
performance question. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu` therefore refuses to probe when the field is not
00, which it cannot do without the register being mapped. Measured on the 6 GiB board
and the 8 GiB board on 2026-07-22: 0x08873034 = 0x00000000, i.e. the DCXO — but that is
boot0's leftover state, not a guarantee, which is the whole reason for the
check. The block itself is 64 KiB, so 0x3038 is well inside it.

