THE ONE OBSERVABLE THAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS PATCH

With `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`+`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu`+`arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` (+`nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` for the OPP table) booted on the 6 GiB board or the 8 GiB board,
drive cluster0 from 1014 MHz to 1794 MHz and, from a core on the *other*
cluster (taskset -c 6, so the observer is not the observed), read with
devmem:

  0x08871000 bits[15:8] must go 0x27 (N=39) -> 0x45 (N=69), and
  0x0887101C bits[26:24] must be observed leaving 0b011 for one of the
  backup sources and coming back to 0b011 around that change,

and a fixed-iteration benchmark pinned to cpu0 must then take 1014/1794 =
0.565x the wall time it took before.

Three distinct ways this fails, each meaning something different:
  - N never changes            -> the clock never reached the PLL: the mux
                                  is not CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, or clocks[0]
                                  did not resolve at all.
  - N changes, mux never moves -> the driver re-locks the PLL underneath a
                                  running core; pointing cpu@N at the mux
                                  bought nothing and the sequence is unsafe
                                  by the UM's own gating step.
  - registers change, wall time does not -> the core is not actually running
                                  on the clock this DT says it runs on, and
                                  the whole mux model is wrong.

